    "slip_type": "variable",
    "smoothing_constraint": true,
    "notes": "Figure 8 CATEGORY DIGITIZATION, not original inversion array. The authors report one 15 x 13 km plane (195 cells at 1 x 1 km), strike 214 degrees, dip 50 degrees NW, average rake -106 degrees, top modeled depth 1.6 km, and peak slip ~1.0 m near depth 6 km. Figure 8 prints SIX INTERVALS: <5, 5–20, 20–40, 40–60, 60–80 and >80 cm; category upper bound for >80 is NOT GIVEN. JSON stores nominal class representatives 0, 12.5, 30, 50, 70, 90 cm. The >80 representative of 90 cm is an explicit estimate informed by the published ~100 cm peak, NOT measured precision; numeric values must not be interpreted as exact slip or used to compute physically rigorous moment. White <5 class coded as 0 cm only as a zero-compatible approximation: 0 is NOT known precisely. The published 15 x 13 modeled subfault extent occupies the first 13 cell rows in the 15-row drawn frame; two all-white bottom frame rows are excluded. The graph arrows indicate variable slip direction and do NOT carry scalar slip data; not digitized. Patch latitude/longitude left null because geographic top-left fault-plane anchor is not supplied; local patch depth is inferred from 1.6 km top and 50-degree dip. Hypocentral depth 10 km comes from NOA data in Table 1 and differs from published ~6 km location of highest slip."
  },
